---
/**
 * Editions (docs/decisions.md D13).
 *
 * This page exists because printed books outlive redesigns. A page number printed in the
 * first run must still resolve after a second run moves it, so the routing is
 * edition-aware and the editions have to be written down somewhere a reader can see.
 *
 * The routed-page count is read from the live page index rather than typed here, so it
 * cannot drift away from what /p/[n] actually resolves.
 */
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import ChapterFurniture from "../components/ChapterFurniture.astro";
import { pageIndex } from "../lib/content";

const index = await pageIndex();
const routed = [...index.keys()].filter((n) => n >= 1 && n <= 64).length;

const EDITION_ONE = [
  { key: "Name", value: "The Condensed Gen Z Edition" },
  { key: "Print run", value: "First, 2026" },
  { key: "Publisher", value: "Bright Kids" },
  { key: "Author", value: "Ryan Rijvers" },
  { key: "Language", value: "English. Written for readers aged 13 and up." },
  { key: "Printed pages", value: "64" },
  { key: "Trim size", value: "140 × 216 mm" },
  { key: "Page routing", value: "/p/1 to /p/64 resolve against this edition." },
];
---

<Page
  title="Editions"
  description="Which print run this site's page numbers refer to, and how a printed page number keeps working after a second edition moves it."
>
  <ChapterFurniture
    eyebrow="The record"
    title="Editions"
    subtitle="Which printed book this site's page numbers belong to — and what happens to them when a second run moves them."
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        Every tool page on this site states the printed page it came from, and you can type
        a page number straight into the homepage to get there. That only works if it is
        clear which book the number is in. This page is that record.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="edition-1">
    <h2 id="edition-1">Edition 1 — the one this site follows</h2>
    <div class="rows">
      {
        EDITION_ONE.map((r) => (
          <div class="row">
            <p class="row__key">{r.key}</p>
            <p class="row__value">{r.value}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
      <div class="row">
        <p class="row__key">ISBN</p>
        <div class="row__value">
          <div data-placeholder class="placeholder">
            <p class="placeholder__label">Not yet known</p>
            <p class="placeholder__note">
              The paperback and ebook ISBNs are not confirmed. They will be listed here,
              in full, so a librarian or a bookseller can match this site to the physical
              object without asking anyone.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="prose note">
      <b>{routed}</b> of the 64 printed pages currently resolve to a tool on this site. The
      rest are story and essay pages, which stay in the book.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="p65">
    <h2 id="p65">Page 65</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The book ends on printed page 64 with “End of the condensed edition.” There is no
        page 65 in the book — but typing 65 here opens{" "}
        <a href="/c/16">Chapter 16</a>, written by Ryan about what he learned after the book
        went to print. It is the one thing a printed book can never contain.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is not a locked room. Typing 65 is the pleasant way to find it; the plain link
        above works just as well, it is in the site's navigation, and search engines can
        read it. A secret that can only be reached by an easter egg excludes anyone using a
        screen reader and anyone who never thinks to try.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="second-run">
    <h2 id="second-run">When there is a second print run</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Page numbers move when a book is reset. That is normal, and it is exactly how a
        printed QR code turns into a dead link two years later. So three rules hold here:
      </p>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>
          <b>The routing is per edition.</b> A second edition gets its own table on this
          page, and its own mapping. The first edition's mapping is never edited to match
          it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>A printed address is permanent.</b> Anything printed in a book is treated as
          immutable forever. <b>/p/1</b> to <b>/p/64</b> must never return a 404, no matter
          how many times this site is rebuilt.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Every page states its own number.</b> If a tool page says “book page 30” and
          your copy says something else, your copy is a different edition — come back here
          and check which.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note">
        Until a second edition exists, everything on this site refers to the first.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="corrections">
    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        If a page number here does not match the book in your hands, that is worth telling
        us: the mailbox at the bottom of this page is read by an adult. Corrections get a
        dated entry on <a href="/updates">the changelog</a> saying what was wrong, rather
        than a silent edit.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-inline-size: 72rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: clamp(1.25rem, 5vw, 3rem);
  }

  .prose {
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
  }

  .prose p + p,
  .prose ul {
    margin-block-start: 0.9em;
  }

  .lede {
    margin-block-start: 1.75rem;
    font-size: var(--text-subtitle);
    line-height: var(--text-subtitle--line-height);
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }

  .section {
    margin-block-start: 3rem;
  }

  .section > h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-h2);
    line-height: var(--text-h2--line-height);
    color: var(--text-section);
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
  }

  .section > .prose {
    margin-block-start: 0.75rem;
  }

  .rows {
    margin-block-start: 1.5rem;
    /* The key column plus the gap plus the measure, so the hairlines end where the words
       do rather than running on to the 72rem wrap. Matches grid-template-columns below. */
    max-inline-size: calc(10rem + 2rem + var(--container-measure));
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--rule-hairline);
  }

  .row {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.25rem 2rem;
    padding-block: 0.9rem;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule-hairline);
  }

  .row__key {
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .row__value {
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
    /* Page numbers, trim sizes and ISBNs are data. */
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums slashed-zero;
  }

  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .row {
      grid-template-columns: 10rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      align-items: baseline;
    }
  }

  [data-placeholder] {
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    background: var(--surface-card);
    border: 1px dashed var(--rule-hairline);
  }

  .placeholder__label {
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: var(--text-label--letter-spacing);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }

  .placeholder__note {
    margin-block-start: 0.35rem;
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .plain {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .plain li {
    padding-block: 0.55rem;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule-hairline);
  }

  .note {
    margin-block-start: 1.25rem;
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .note b {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums slashed-zero;
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }

  a {
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
</style>
